---

<DocsLayout
  title="CLI Reference"
  subtitle="Command surface defined in packages/ragu/src/index.ts and packages/ragu/src/cli/cmd."
  description="Reference for the ragu CLI command surface, default TUI mode, flags, and command execution behavior."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="entry-point" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Entry Point</h2>
    <p>
      Running <code>ragu</code> without a subcommand opens the default terminal UI (TUI) in the
      current directory. Pass an optional project path to open the TUI in another directory. Global
      flags <code>--print-logs</code> and <code>--log-level</code> apply to every command.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      ragu                    # open TUI in current directory
      ragu /path/to/project   # open TUI in another project
      ragu --help             # list all commands
      ragu completion         # generate shell completion script
    </DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Default TUI">
      <p>
        The default TUI command is <code>$0 [project]</code> and accepts session, model, agent,
        continue, prompt, and network flags. All options available to <code>ragu run</code> are
        also accepted here.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="interactive-workflows" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Interactive Workflows</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Command</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu [project]</code></td>
          <td>Open the TUI in the current or specified project directory.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu run [message..]</code></td>
          <td>Run a one-shot prompt without entering the TUI.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu run --attach &lt;url&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Connect a one-shot run to a running ragu server. <code>--attach</code> is a flag on <code>run</code>, not a separate command.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu session list</code></td>
          <td>List past and active sessions.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu quickstart</code></td>
          <td>Interactive walkthrough for first-time users. Accepts <code>--skip-checks</code> and <code>--dry-run</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu pr &lt;number&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Fetch and checkout a GitHub PR branch, then launch ragu in the resulting worktree.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      ragu run "audit this repo and summarize risks"
      ragu run --attach http://127.0.0.1:4096 "summarize the open work"
      ragu quickstart --skip-checks
      ragu pr 42
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="autonomous-scheduled" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Autonomous &amp; Scheduled</h2>
    <p>
      <code>goal</code> drives the agent autonomously until a verifiable condition is met.
      <code>routine</code> manages saved prompts with scheduled (cron) or API triggers.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Command</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu goal [condition..]</code></td>
          <td>
            Work autonomously until the stated condition is met. Flags: <code>--continue</code>,
            <code>--session</code>, <code>--model</code>, <code>--agent</code>,
            <code>--variant</code>, <code>--token-budget</code>, <code>--format</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu routine list</code></td>
          <td>List all routines for the current project.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu routine create</code></td>
          <td>Create a routine interactively or with flags. See the <a href="/docs/routines">Routines</a> page.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu routine get &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Show details of a specific routine.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu routine run &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Trigger an immediate run of a routine.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu routine pause &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Pause a routine (disables its scheduled triggers).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu routine resume &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Resume a paused routine.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu routine delete &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Delete a routine (alias: <code>rm</code>). Prompts for confirmation unless <code>--yes</code> is passed.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      ragu goal "all tests pass and no lint errors"
      ragu goal --token-budget 50000 "fix the failing integration test"
      ragu routine create --name "daily review" --prompt "summarize recent changes" --cron @daily
      ragu routine run rtn_abc123
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="missions" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Missions</h2>
    <p>
      A <a href="/docs/missions">Mission</a> is a high-altitude workflow that decomposes a goal
      into milestones, each holding a DAG of features, with a validation checkpoint per
      milestone. <code>ragu mission</code> is a thin headless wrapper over the mission
      orchestrator (<code>packages/ragu/src/mission/</code>).
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu mission</code></td>
          <td>Open the headless mission manager: list, status, quick actions.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu mission new [-f file] [--brief …] [--model …] [--agent …]</code></td>
          <td>
            Create a mission from a brief. With <code>-f</code> the brief is read from a file;
            otherwise it is read from <code>--brief</code> or stdin. <code>--model</code> and
            <code>--agent</code> set the worker defaults.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu mission start &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Begin orchestrating the mission. The mission moves from <code>pending</code> to <code>running</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu mission pause &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Pause a running mission. In-flight features finish; no new features start.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu mission resume &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Resume a paused mission.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu mission cancel &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Cancel the mission. In-flight workers are aborted via <code>prompt.cancel</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu mission get &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Show progress, current milestone, current feature, statuses, and last error.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu mission list</code></td>
          <td>List all missions in the current project with their runtime status.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      # Draft a mission from a brief file and start it
      ragu mission new -f ./brief.md --agent build --model anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5
      ragu mission start mss_abc123
      ragu mission get mss_abc123
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="loops" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Loops</h2>
    <p>
      A <a href="/docs/loops">Loop</a> is a named, persisted workflow that runs an ordered list
      of stages, each of which is a single <code>goal</code> command. The headless LoopEngine
      owns the interval timers, so loops keep running when the TUI is closed.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu loop list</code></td>
          <td>List all loops for the current project.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu loop create</code></td>
          <td>Create a loop interactively or with flags. Flags: <code>--name</code>, <code>--stage</code> (repeatable), <code>--every</code>, <code>--max-runs</code>, <code>--timeout</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu loop get &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Show full definition, runtime status, and the last few runs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu loop run &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Trigger an immediate run. Accepts <code>--text</code> to append one-off context to this run.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu loop pause &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Stop firing triggers. In-flight runs are not aborted.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu loop resume &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Re-enable the loop's triggers.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu loop cancel &lt;run-id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Cancel an in-flight run.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ragu loop delete &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Delete the loop and its run history. Prompts for confirmation unless <code>--yes</code> is passed.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      # Two-stage loop that triages issues then drafts a fix every 30 minutes
      ragu loop create --name "triage-and-fix" \
        --stage "triage:plan:Triage open issues and rank by impact" \
        --stage "draft-fix:build:Open a draft PR for the top issue" \
        --every 30m

      ragu loop run lop_abc123
      ragu loop pause lop_abc123
      ragu loop delete lop_abc123 --yes
    </DocCode>
  </section>

